WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:01.150
Edward Cullen.

00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:02.360
Han Solo.

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:03.360
Killmoklknger.

00:00:03.360 --> 00:00:04.360
Lestat.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:08.200
What do all these characters have in common
besides being heartthrobs?

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:11.070
They share a common ancestor: the Byronic
Hero.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:17.090
Brooding, sensual, violent, intelligent, and
single-minded, the Byronic hero has been a

00:00:17.090 --> 00:00:22.320
staple in literature dating back to the 19th
century, but the archetype is all over film,

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.339
TV and even video games.

00:00:24.339 --> 00:00:28.999
I see you Cloud Strife, all sad and angsty
with your giant sword.

00:00:28.999 --> 00:00:32.410
But what does it mean to be a Byronic hero?

00:00:32.410 --> 00:00:35.500
And who was the man that helped create this
iconic archetype?

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:40.940
Let’s get into the myth, the man, and the
literary legacy.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:48.800
According to Professor Peter L. Thorslev,
author of The Byronic Hero: Types and Prototypes,

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.520
and Romantic Contraries,

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:55.890
“The characteristic Byronic hero. . .has
borrowed characteristics from the Gothic Villain,

00:00:55.890 --> 00:01:01.690
in his looks, his mysterious past, and his
secret sins; and[...] from the Man of Feeling

00:01:01.690 --> 00:01:08.290
in his tender sensibilities and in his undying
fidelity to the woman he loves—but he is

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:14.990
more than these: he is also a Romantic rebel.[...]
he chooses his values in open defiance of

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:16.060
the codes of society”

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:22.620
That’s right you defy the codes of society
by being … sad and hot, with your slightly

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:26.310
stalker-like tendencies.

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:30.920
The Byronic Hero allowed for more complicated
male characters to form and without him we

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:33.070
miss out on the development of the anti-hero.

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:36.780
But where did the Byronic hero even come from?

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:42.640
A very, very bad boy named George Gordon Byron
aka Lord Byron.

00:01:42.640 --> 00:01:49.420
Born to an absent father and a mercurial mother,
Byron had a brilliant mind, but was tormented

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:52.530
due to being born with misshapen foot.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:58.119
He came into wealth while young, but was surrounded
by such a malevolent cast of characters in

00:01:58.119 --> 00:02:01.950
his home life, he makes The Dursleys look
loving and supportive.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:08.209
Because of his slightly warped foot, Byron
exercised excessively to make up for what

00:02:08.209 --> 00:02:09.929
he saw as a flaw.

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:15.290
The other thing he was excessive about, was
his love of the ladies (and the gents).

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:21.829
Enter: Lady Caroline Lamb, England, 1812.

00:02:21.829 --> 00:02:27.739
Caroline Lamb, wife of future Prime Minister
Lord Melbourne, had a very messy love affair

00:02:27.739 --> 00:02:33.650
with Byron that had several public dramatic
moments, including her breaking a wine glass

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:36.000
and trying to harm herself.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.180
She notoriously called Byron: “mad, bad,
and dangerous to know.”

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:46.140
Definite a gemini, TMZ would have lived for it.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:51.860
The real kicker came when Lamb wrote her gothic
revenge novel Glenarvon, which frames Byron

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:57.669
as a vampiric figure who, through his incredible
magnetism, attracts women only to suck out

00:02:57.669 --> 00:03:04.019
their strength by stealing their hearts, and taking their innocence.

00:03:04.019 --> 00:03:09.290
Glenarvon was basically a tell-all book under
the guise of fiction with very clear allusions

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:15.810
to real people, allusions that were so obvious
that it ended Lamb’s standing in society

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:16.950
…. forever.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:22.239
But the public loved it and the main villain
that Byron inspired, Clarence de Ruthven aka

00:03:22.239 --> 00:03:25.620
Lord Glenarvon, was … deliciously messy.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:32.349
It was from this story that we got the first,
non-Byron created, Byronic “hero.”

00:03:32.349 --> 00:03:35.799
And lo, the Byronic floodgates opened.

00:03:35.799 --> 00:03:40.239
Gothic and romantic fiction of the 19th and
early 20th century ate this up.

00:03:40.239 --> 00:03:48.889
The Brontes, Dumas, Hugo, Leroux, hell even
Ian Fleming’s James Bond is pretty Byronic.

00:03:48.889 --> 00:03:52.530
So why is this version of a romantic hero
so popular?

00:03:52.530 --> 00:03:57.269
There are many kinds of Byronic Heroes, but
for the sake of everyone we are going to split

00:03:57.269 --> 00:04:01.839
them into two groups: the Gothic and the Romantic.

00:04:01.839 --> 00:04:08.110
Gothic novels which often blend together horror
and romance feature dark hopeless figures

00:04:08.110 --> 00:04:13.949
tormented by a wrong done to them that makes
up their entire existence.

00:04:13.949 --> 00:04:20.060
They can sometimes be villains, but are often
anti-heroes possessing some sort of complex

00:04:20.060 --> 00:04:23.879
emotional backstory meant to be sympathetic.

00:04:23.879 --> 00:04:28.500
Some famous examples are Victor Frankenstein
and Frankenstein’s monster (Frankenstein),

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:34.669
Captain Ahab (Moby Dick), Erik (Phantom of
the Opera), and Edmond Dantès (The Count

00:04:34.669 --> 00:04:42.660
of Monte Cristo)...and Megamind!

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:48.501
In Romance literature (ie Romance literature
of the 19th century as opposed to the romance

00:04:48.501 --> 00:04:53.900
genre we know today) the hero is a solitary
figure who seeks to live out their life in

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:58.569
isolation, but is pulled into society against
their will.

00:04:58.569 --> 00:05:03.020
They have a mixture of monstrous appearances,
yet alluring personalities.

00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:10.370
They will yell at you once, and then stare
longing into your eyes afterwards.

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:13.940
... It’s kind of a red flag if you think
about it.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:19.440
Popular versions of this are Rochester from
Jane Eyre, Heathcliff from Wuthering Heights,

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:24.750
and ... pretty much any vampire in fiction
who was ever sad about being a vampire.

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:26.270
They love to brood!

00:05:26.270 --> 00:05:33.159
In the sad boy vs vengeance boy venn diagram
what connects them is the grey morality that

00:05:33.159 --> 00:05:37.330
makes you as the reader empathize with their
situation.

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:43.050
The Creature from Frankenstein is reacting
to the cruel world that created him and Victor,

00:05:43.050 --> 00:05:50.010
while reckless, was genuinely frightened by
what he had done and didn’t deserve to have

00:05:50.010 --> 00:05:51.670
his whole family slaughtered.

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:56.850
Edmound Dantèes’ desire for revenge after
spending fourteen years in a French prison

00:05:56.850 --> 00:06:02.050
after being framed for a crime he didn’t
commit is very understandable.

00:06:02.050 --> 00:06:08.180
However, does it actually justify the innocent
lives lost in the crossfire of said revenge

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:09.180
quest?

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:16.159
Judge Claude Frollo from Victor Hugo’s 1831
novel Hunchback is a good man who loves his

00:06:16.159 --> 00:06:25.189
adoptive son and younger brother, but is gripped
with lust for a woman he can not have and

00:06:25.189 --> 00:06:32.760
it tips him into madness quicker than you
can say “I’m losing to a bird.”

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:37.639
Heathcliff is such a compelling romantic lead
because the text makes it clear he was forced

00:06:37.639 --> 00:06:45.110
into becoming a bitter hateful man, but his
young toxic love for Catherine and his lack

00:06:45.110 --> 00:06:49.590
of redemption make him oddly sympathetic.

00:06:49.590 --> 00:06:57.060
Rochester has this deep love for Jane and
treats his servants kindly, but locks his

00:06:57.060 --> 00:07:01.180
mentally ill wife in an attic and shames her
for being sick.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:06.980
Byron, he had this huge capacity for love, intelligence, and understood

00:07:06.980 --> 00:07:10.980
the great beauty in the world.

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:13.940
But, also chaotic and emotionally aloof.

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:18.940
He was the baddest
bad boy and the appeal of that character is

00:07:18.949 --> 00:07:22.759
that misguided hope that one day you will
be the one.

00:07:22.759 --> 00:07:25.189
That you might ~ change him.

00:07:25.189 --> 00:07:26.189
~

00:07:26.189 --> 00:07:30.590
As literary genre critic, Conrad Aquilina
eloquently put it: “The Byronic hero bears

00:07:30.590 --> 00:07:33.570
the dual markings of both villain and victim.

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:38.969
He is a fallen creature in his own right;
a dark angel bringing both love and death,

00:07:38.969 --> 00:07:42.340
yearning for redemption and ultimately finding
none.”

00:07:42.340 --> 00:07:48.599
Erik, the eponymous phantom from Gaston Leroux’s
Phantom of the Opera is a talented man whose

00:07:48.599 --> 00:07:56.480
lifetime of pain has forced him to resort
to extreme means in order to be loved by someone.

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:03.789
Like building a torture chamber right to his
guest bedroom.

00:08:03.789 --> 00:08:08.240
In EM Hull’s 1919 novel The Sheik - not
only is our hero abusive and tortured (which

00:08:08.240 --> 00:08:14.320
I am putting mildly) he breaks his victim
so hard she totally falls in love with him

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:15.569
by the end.

00:08:15.569 --> 00:08:19.849
But don’t worry, he feels bad about it eventually.

00:08:19.849 --> 00:08:26.259
This novel, though incredibly ~problematic~
created a paradigm shift for the Byronic hero

00:08:26.259 --> 00:08:30.650
and romance in general - before this, if a
woman is “defiled”, she can only regain

00:08:30.650 --> 00:08:36.720
her purity through death, but this time she…
gets a happy ending?

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:39.770
With the guy who kidnapped her?

00:08:39.770 --> 00:08:40.770
Winning?

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:47.940
Though not as hot of a commodity in the mid-20th
century, the late 20th century saw a huge

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:52.760
resurgence in the popularity of the Byronic
hero - there was the emergence of the bodice

00:08:52.760 --> 00:08:58.750
ripper subgenre of romance novels like 1972’s
The Flame and the Flower, which is considered

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:03.120
the first “bodice ripper” romance and
revolutionized the modern romance genre, and

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:09.520
it has a dynamic between its two romantic
leads that is… very influenced by the Sheik.

00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:14.230
And then there’s more recent characters
like Twilight’s Edward Cullen, and his spiritual

00:09:14.230 --> 00:09:17.000
descendant Christian Grey (ugh).

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:23.590
Yeah, I know, but he’s a really popular
example so we have to talk about him.

00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:29.520
Grey has severe childhood emotional and sexual
trauma that keeps him from being able to form

00:09:29.520 --> 00:09:35.060
healthy sexual or romantic relationships with
women he respects.

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:38.590
Or … able to respect women period.

00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:45.150
But don’t worry, here comes bright-eyed
virgin Anastasia Steele, whom he gaslights,

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:48.440
abuses and makes her cry a lot.

00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:55.650
She wants to change him and by the end of
the thousand-plus agonizing pages, she succeeds,

00:09:55.650 --> 00:10:00.900
and he’s healed now, hooray.

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:05.210
These characters are all different from the
more traditional, upbeat capital R - romantic

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:12.320
hero in that they are primarily shaped and
motivated by their traumas, past and present.

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:16.780
One might note that overwhelmingly these characters
are male and white.

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:21.730
But more recently we see more female characters
who possess some Byronic qualities: Faith

00:10:21.730 --> 00:10:27.940
from Buffy the Vampire Slayer, Regina from
Once Upon a Time, Olivia Pope from Scandal,

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:34.290
Catra from She-Ra, but those characters are
punished a lot more by both audiences and

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:35.960
writers for their nature.

00:10:35.960 --> 00:10:42.290
It tends not to be framed with the same seductive
quality.

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:46.240
The alluring aspect of female and non-white
Byronic characters is seeing them have the

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:51.500
freedom to be more complex than just idealized
one-note types of representation.

00:10:51.500 --> 00:10:56.580
Atara Stein argues that Catherine Earnshaw
from Wuthering Heights and Eustacia Vye from

00:10:56.580 --> 00:11:01.780
The Return of the Native by Thomas Hardy are
Byronic heroes because of their “rebellion

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:07.600
against a conventional society that stifles
individuality” and that “these Byronic

00:11:07.600 --> 00:11:14.950
heroines take on the characteristics of the
rebellious, ambitious, narcissistic, individualistic,

00:11:14.950 --> 00:11:18.240
and ultimately self-destructive Byronic male.”

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:22.730
However both these female characters are killed
off and those who are not, end up finding

00:11:22.730 --> 00:11:28.910
their way back to “traditional female values.”

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:33.990
This reflects the reality for a lot of women
in history who were both brilliant, but were

00:11:33.990 --> 00:11:42.730
also loud, and may have suffered from mental
illness and didn’t conduct themselves as

00:11:42.730 --> 00:11:43.730
“ladies”

00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:48.480
A major difference between the male and female
variants, at least in the 19th century, is

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:54.050
that these female characters are not allowed
to be romanticized in the same way men are.

00:11:54.050 --> 00:12:00.140
Byronic heroes are unlikeable on purpose,
but there will always be a privilege of being

00:12:00.140 --> 00:12:04.970
white, male, and rich that allowed these characters
to be so awful and get away with it.

00:12:04.970 --> 00:12:10.060
Do you think Rochester would be able to get
away with having a wife in his attic for the

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:13.190
lulz if he wasn’t a white dude?

00:12:13.190 --> 00:12:17.290
Byron died at 36 years old from an illness
exacerbated by the then-common medical practice

00:12:17.290 --> 00:12:18.290
of bloodletting.

00:12:18.290 --> 00:12:20.220
I'll let you google that.

00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:23.140
His tragic death at a young age made him a hero.

00:12:23.140 --> 00:12:29.630
The world mourned him in the same way people
mourned Hendrix, Cobain, Winehouse, the sudden

00:12:29.630 --> 00:12:36.660
death of a great artistic genius who burned
so hotly and wildly that it was no shock at

00:12:36.660 --> 00:12:38.670
all he burned out so young.

00:12:38.670 --> 00:12:42.350
But in a delightful touch of irony one of
the greatest legacies of Byron’s life was

00:12:42.350 --> 00:12:48.890
his daughter Ada Lovelace, called one of the
first computer programmers and some might

00:12:48.890 --> 00:12:54.490
say even more of a genius than her own father.

00:12:54.490 --> 00:12:59.780
And according to British literature Professor
Andrew Elfenbein, “Byron is

00:12:59.780 --> 00:13:04.020
not just an author, but an unprecedented cultural
phenomenon.

00:13:04.020 --> 00:13:10.340
His work affects not only the novel, poetry,
and drama, but fashion, social manners, erotic

00:13:10.340 --> 00:13:12.460
experience, and gender roles.”

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:17.500
The Byronic Hero has much in common with the
broader concept of the anti-hero: tormented

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:22.380
by forces beyond their control, with a sharp
wit that they have developed in order to cope

00:13:22.380 --> 00:13:26.250
with whatever monstrous thing is innate to
them, be it vampirism, childhood trauma or

00:13:26.250 --> 00:13:27.250
a weird foot.

00:13:27.250 --> 00:13:28.710
Now this damage doesn’t make them good.

00:13:28.710 --> 00:13:43.930
There is no good reason to emotionally manipulate
people or to lock your sick wife in the attic,

00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:46.640
but the cruel circumstances of fate make them
tragic.

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:51.840
Especially when the story makes it clear that
otherwise they might have actually been someone

00:13:51.840 --> 00:13:53.980
capable of true emotional growth.

00:13:53.980 --> 00:13:58.910
Leroux even ends his The Phantom of the Opera
thusly - “With an ordinary face, he would

00:13:58.910 --> 00:14:02.190
have been one of the noblest members of the
human race.

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:07.081
He had a heart great enough to hold the empire
of the world, and in the end he had to be

00:14:07.081 --> 00:14:08.570
content with a cellar.”

00:14:08.570 --> 00:14:14.440
All of the tortured romantic bad boys of literature,
film, and television have a little bit of

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:15.440
Byron in them.

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:21.510
So next time you get deep in your feelings
for Kylo Ren or cheer on the redemption of

00:14:21.510 --> 00:14:27.320
Prince Zuko or secretly pop on Twilight for
the 200th time, pour one out for Lord Byron,

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:30.230
to whom we owe so much.

00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:44.680
Yet we still formed an entire romantic based
around who he was, flaws and all.

